{
  "description": "The freshness half for `ask`, whose second entry is NOT the one its `each` sibling uses. `each` runs its body once per addressed turtle, so a second turtle is a second entry; `ask` runs its body once for the whole addressed set, so its second entry is a second EXECUTION of the statement — here a second turn of the enclosing `repeat`. Each entry still creates a fresh scope with fresh bindings (spec/execution-model.md:377-379,664-666), so the second entry must not find the `:carried` the first bound, and the read raises `ol-undefined-var` (spec/error-model.md:102). Added at the QA reviewer's request during review round 3, which measured that sharing one frame across `ask` body entries survives all 1035 fixtures: the round-2 fixtures gave `ask` the death half only, and generalising `each`'s second-entry recipe to `ask` is exactly how the gap was missed. This fixture has no check: true twin on purpose: check() does flag the same read, but positionally — the read precedes the write in source order — and never by freshness, which is a property a single static walk of the block cannot express. A twin would pin the already-pinned positional rule (check/undefined-var-eager-block-reads-a-later-enclosing-binding) while reading as though it pinned this one.",
